course: everyday
lesson: 204
title: Pranks: harmless fun or real harm?
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A good prank is one where everybody laughs afterwards, including the person pranked, and nobody is hurt, frightened for real, embarrassed in front of many people or left with a mess. A whoopee cushion usually passes this test.
explain: Before any prank, ask: Could anyone get hurt? Is this person truly afraid of it? Will something be damaged or wasted? Will they be humiliated? Would they agree to it? If any answer worries you, do not do it.
explain: Some pranks are never okay: pulling a chair away as someone sits (they can hurt their back or head), tricking someone about food they are allergic to (allergies can kill), pranking animals, damaging property, prank phone calls, and ringing doorbells and running away.
explain: Teasing works the same way: the other person's feelings decide whether it is still fun. Look at their face and ask «Are you okay with this?»
explain: Example: a girl in Auckland hides a rubber snake in her brother's drawer, and he screams and cries, because he is truly scared of snakes. She stops, says sorry and helps him calm down. A prank that hurts someone is no longer a joke.
fact: The other person's feelings decide whether teasing is still fun; look at their face and ask.
fact: Everyone can like what they like, and teasing about interests makes people hide what they enjoy.
fact: Never pull a chair away as someone sits; they can fall and badly hurt their back or head.
fact: A harmless prank is one where everyone laughs, including the person pranked, and nobody is harmed.
fact: A prank that spoils someone's food or day is not funny to them.
fact: A sudden fright can make people fall or feel unwell, so never scare people near stairs or roads, or people who are frail or have a heart condition.
fact: For someone with a real fear, like of spiders or snakes, a fake one can cause panic rather than fun.
fact: Prank calls can scare or annoy people, especially older people living alone.
fact: Being laughed at by many people online can be very humiliating, so do not film pranks without agreement.
fact: Damaging property in a prank means someone must clean or pay for it, and it can be a crime.
fact: Animals do not understand jokes; pranks frighten, stress or hurt them.
fact: When a prank goes wrong and someone is upset, stop, say sorry and help.
fact: Never trick anyone about what is in their food, because food allergies can kill.
exercise: You are not sure if your teasing has gone too far. What should you do? | Look at the other person's face and ask «Are you okay with this?» | Keep going until they tell a teacher, then you know it was too much | Tease a bit harder to see whether they laugh along this time | Ask your other friends whether they still think it is funny
exercise: Some boys tease a classmate for liking dancing. What should you do? | Not join in, because everyone can like what they like | Join in, since everybody knows dancing is only for girls | Tell him quietly he should choose a different hobby | Laugh quietly behind your hand so he does not notice
exercise: A friend wants to pull a chair away as someone sits down. Why never do it? | The person can fall and badly hurt their back or head | The chair might get scratched badly on the hard floor | The teacher will see it happen and be very cross with you | It is only dangerous for grown-ups, not for children
exercise: You put a whoopee cushion on a friend's seat and she laughs too. Is that okay? | Yes, a harmless prank where everyone laughs is fine | No, all pranks are always wrong, even the silly ones | Only if you do it to her every single day of the week | Only if you film it and show it to the whole class later
exercise: A friend wants to put salt in the sugar bowl. What should you think about? | Who will be fooled, whether they will be upset, and wasted food | Only whether it will make you and your friend laugh a lot at breakfast | Whether salt and sugar look exactly the same in the bowl | Whether your parents are at home to see the joke happen
exercise: When is jumping out to scare someone especially dangerous? | Near stairs or roads, or with someone frail or with a heart condition | Only when it is dark outside and nobody can see you coming towards them | Only when the person you scare is exactly the same age as you | It is never dangerous to scare anyone, it only makes them jump
exercise: Your sister is truly afraid of spiders. Is a fake spider in her drawer a good prank? | No, for someone with a real fear it causes panic, not fun | Yes, a fright like that will help her get over the fear | Yes, because the spider is plastic, so it cannot hurt her | Only if you hide it so well she finds it days later
exercise: Why should you not make prank phone calls to strangers? | They can scare or annoy people, especially older people alone | Phone calls to strangers cost your family far too much money each time | Strangers always know straight away who called them and why | It is only wrong at night, when people are already asleep
exercise: A friend wants to film a prank on a classmate and post it online. What is right? | Do not prank people for videos without their agreement | Post it, as long as it is funny and gets plenty of likes | Post it, but blur the classmate's face a little first | Only post it to your close friends, so it stays secret
exercise: Some older children talk about egging a house. What is true? | Damaging property is never a prank and can be a crime | It is fine at Halloween, because that is what the night is for | It is fine if the eggs are old and would be thrown away | Only the person who threw the egg can get into trouble
exercise: Someone wants to scare the family dog for a laugh. What is true? | Never prank animals; they get frightened, stressed or hurt | Dogs enjoy being scared now and then, it keeps them alert | It is fine if the dog is big enough not to be frightened | Animals understand jokes just the way people do, so it's fine
exercise: Children want to play «ding dong ditch». Why not? | It can frighten people, and running away near roads is dangerous | Doorbells break if you ring them too many times in one single evening | It is only wrong if you ring the same bell twice in a row | The people inside will remember your face and chase you forever
exercise: A prank goes wrong and your friend cries. What should you do? | Stop, say sorry and help her | Tell her it was only a joke, nothing more | Laugh so she sees it was funny | Walk away until she feels better
exercise: Your class wants to prank the teacher. What is wise? | Keep it very gentle, or skip it | Do something big so everyone remembers | Hide all her things before class | Make her look silly in front of everyone
exercise: Someone suggests hiding peanuts in the food of a child with a peanut allergy as a trick. What is true? | Never trick anyone about food; allergies can kill | A tiny amount is fine as a joke, it cannot do harm | It is fine as long as you tell them straight afterwards | Allergies only make people a bit itchy for a while
test: A boy in Denver teases his friend for liking football, saying it is only for boys. Why is this unkind? | Everyone can like what they like, and teasing makes people hide it | Because football really is a game meant only for girls to play in teams | It is not unkind at all if it is said with a friendly smile | It is only unkind if the friend plays badly and loses the match
test: A girl plans to jump out at her grandad, who has a heart problem, at the top of the stairs. What is the danger? | The fright could make him fall or feel unwell | He might not notice her at all and just walk on by | He might jump out at her the next time she comes | There is no danger if she only shouts «boo» softly
test: Which prank is most likely harmless? | A whoopee cushion for a friend who loves silly jokes | Swapping a friend's sugar for salt before her breakfast | A rubber snake for a classmate who is scared of snakes | Filming a friend falling over and then posting it online
test: Ben's prank makes his little sister cry. Ben says, «But it's only a joke!» What is true? | A prank that hurts someone is no longer a joke | It is still a joke because Ben meant it as one | His sister should learn to take a joke | Crying shows she found it funny
test: Children stuff toilet paper into a school toilet as a prank. Why is this wrong? | Someone has to clean or pay for it, and it can be a crime | Toilet paper is far too expensive for a school to waste on a joke | It only matters if the toilet floods the whole corridor | It is fine as long as nobody sees them do it or tells
test: A boy wants to secretly film his cousin being pranked to post online. What should he ask himself first? | Whether his cousin would agree to being shown to many people | Whether the video will get lots of likes | Whether the camera has enough battery | Whether his cousin will find out who posted it
test: Why is pranking an animal never fair? | Animals cannot understand it is a joke and get frightened | Animals do not mind being frightened, they forget it at once | It is only unfair to small animals, not to big ones | Animals always get their revenge on you a bit later
test: A boy phones random numbers and pretends to be a policeman. Who might he upset most? | An older person living alone who is frightened | Nobody at all, since it is all just pretend | Only the real police officers who hear about it | Only people whose phones do not show caller ID
test: Your friend says she cannot eat eggs because of an allergy. Someone hides egg in her cake as a trick. Why is this so serious? | Food allergies can kill, so tricks with food are never okay | Eggs make cakes taste much worse, so she will spit it out at once | She will be angry for a whole day and not speak to anyone | It is only serious if she eats the whole slice of the cake
test: gonna pull his chair away when he sits lol | No, he could fall and hurt his back or head | Fine if there's a soft carpet under the chair | Fine if he laughs about it after, no harm done | Fine if you're quick enough to catch him
test: wanna put a fake spider on my sis, she's terrified of them | No, it can cause real panic, not fun | Yes, it'll help her get over it for good | Yes, if it's a small spider | Yes, if you film it for later
test: put salt in his drink as a prank, he's got allergies tho | Never trick anyone about food, allergies can kill | Salt is fine, it's not a nut, so nothing'll happen | Fine if he only takes a tiny little sip of it | Fine if you tell him straight after he drinks it
test: prank went wrong, she's crying | Stop, say sorry and help | Tell her it was just a joke | Laugh it off to make it lighter | Do another one to cheer her up
explain: A boy in Florida pulled a chair away as his friend sat down; the friend hit his head on the floor, was knocked out for a moment and had to go to hospital.
explain: Teenagers in Glasgow made a fake 999 call as a prank; an ambulance went to the address while a real patient across town had to wait longer for help.
fact: Pulling chairs, tripping and pushing people into water are "pranks" that cause broken bones and head injuries.
fact: Prank calls to 911, 999, 000 or 111 tie up help that someone in real danger needs, and they are against the law.
test: gonna pull Max's chair away when he sits lol | No, he could hit his head and get badly hurt | Yes, it's a classic joke | Yes, if there's a carpet | Only if you film it
test: Why is a fake call to the emergency number a serious thing? | It can keep help away from someone really in danger | The call costs your parents a great deal of money | The phone line breaks for a while afterwards | It is only a problem if you call on a birthday or holiday
test: dared to push my sister in the pool with her clothes on, she can't swim well | No, she could drown, never push anyone in | Yes, it's just a laugh | Yes, if you jump in after | Only in the shallow end
test: did u hear?? a kid pulled a chair away and the boy fell and cracked his head, ambulance came, blood everywhere. real lesson? | Never pull a chair away; falls cause real injuries | Only pull chairs on soft carpet | Warn the person a second before you pull it | Chair pranks are fine for strong kids
test: apparently some boys prank-called 999 like 50 times and the police came to their house and arrested their mum. what's likely blown up? | The mum's arrest; but prank emergency calls are illegal | That police take such calls seriously | That emergency calls tie up real help | That the boys got into trouble
test: my mate says a girl put peanut butter in her friend's sandwich as a joke and the friend nearly died cos allergic. should we film the next prank like that? | No, never trick anyone about food; allergies can kill | Yes, if the friend is not allergic to peanuts | Yes, it makes a great video | Yes, if the food is only a small amount
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a harmless prank? | Everyone, including the person pranked, laughs and none is harmed | Only the persons carrying out the prank are amused by its outcome | Some minor injury is acceptable, provided all laugh later | The person pranked is not informed of the prank afterwards
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a person ought never to be deceived as to the contents of his food. | Food allergies can cause death | Food deceptions are seldom amusing | Food may thereby be wasted | The cook may be offended
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning hoax calls to emergency numbers. | They occupy help needed by persons in real danger and are unlawful | They are harmless provided that the caller hangs up the telephone promptly | They are permitted for persons under sixteen years of age | They are lawful if made only once by the same person
exam: Ascertain the proper course of action when a prank has gone wrong and its target is upset. | To desist, apologise and render assistance | To repeat the prank to show it was a joke | To depart before an adult arrives | To film the target's reaction
